TARA v0.3

A Trajectory-Level, Two-Axis Probabilistic Framework for Separating Hazard Consequences from Asset-Recovery Outcomes in Reusable Launch Vehicle Capture Operations — with Repositioned Novelty, Mechanism-Indexed Classification, and Decision-Relevance Testing

Author: Ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM

Date: 18 November 2026

Version: 0.3 — Revised research proposal incorporating regulatory baseline audit, mechanism-indexed partition, two-tier uncertainty, state-feedback decision layer, and case-study–anchored falsification

Status: Theoretical doctoral research proposal — mathematical definitions revised; no experimental or numerical validation is claimed. Pilot literature and regulatory findings are reported in Chapter 4.5.

---

Abstract

TARA (Two-Axis Recovery Assessment) addresses a specific assessment problem in reusable launch vehicle (RLV) recovery: whether the physical outcome of a capture attempt can be used as a sufficient statistic for receptor-specific hazard consequences and for asset-recovery outcomes. Version 0.2 established the formal separation of these two axes. Version 0.3 responds to three substantive criticisms of that formulation. First, the novelty claim of v0.2 was overstated: 14 CFR 450 already disaggregates collective risk, individual risk, aircraft risk, and risk to critical assets, and NASA Probabilistic Risk Assessment (PRA) practice already distinguishes loss-of-crew (LOC) from loss-of-vehicle (LOV) end states. The revised contribution is therefore repositioned toward the joint dependence structure between the axes and toward a granularity-deficiency test of the physical partition, not toward the bare fact of separation. Second, the product-space classification of v0.2 is replaced by a mechanism-indexed partition: classes are defined by the dominant hazard-generating mechanism, not by combinations of terminal integrity states. Third, the decision layer is reformulated as a state-feedback stopping-time problem with distributionally robust receptor constraints, acknowledging that abort decisions are made in real time under partial observation, not as batch optimizations over a fixed policy set.

The central hypothesis is revised accordingly. TARA v0.3 does not test whether separation is conceptually possible; it tests whether separation changes the admissibility or ranking of recovery policies by a margin that exceeds the epistemic uncertainty of the underlying physical and consequence models. A negative result—separation is decision-relevant only under specified conditions and is otherwise dominated by model uncertainty—is treated as a legitimate scientific outcome.

