Chapter 3 — Hypotheses and Falsification (Revised)

The v0.2 existence hypotheses (H2, H3) are replaced by decision-relevance and prevalence hypotheses. Existence is too weak a bar; the revised hypotheses can fail meaningfully.

H1 — Mechanism-indexed classification sufficiency

Hypothesis: A mechanism-indexed partition \{\Omega_{m_j}\} produces classes that are more hazard-homogeneous than the principal regimes of v0.2, measured by the within-class spread of P(B_r \mid \Omega_k).

Falsification: If the mechanism-indexed partition does not reduce within-class hazard spread relative to the v0.2 eight-class partition across the declared scenario set, the mechanism-indexing adds no diagnostic value.

H2 — Decision-relevance of separation

Hypothesis: There exists at least one representative policy set and operating domain in which \Delta_{\mathrm{rev}} is smaller than the Tier-1 + Tier-2 uncertainty band, so that the two-axis assessment reverses a policy ranking that the aggregate baseline does not reverse.

Falsification: If \Delta_{\mathrm{rev}} exceeds the uncertainty band for every scenario in the declared set, separation is not decision-relevant within that domain, and the practical contribution must be narrowed to reporting and diagnosis rather than decision change.

H3 — Joint dependence magnitude

Hypothesis: The joint exceedance probability P(H \in \mathcal{B}_H, A \in \mathcal{B}_A) differs from the product of marginals by a margin that exceeds the Monte Carlo confidence interval for at least one receptor–asset pair, under the physical model.

Falsification: If the dependence is statistically indistinguishable from independence for every receptor–asset pair across the declared domain, the joint modeling requirement is not practically necessary, and the two axes can be treated as approximately separable for that domain.

H4 — Trajectory-level hazard detection

Hypothesis: At least one transient post-capture hazard mechanism (e.g., tower load during stabilization, propellant safing thermal transient) produces H_r^{\mathrm{trajectory}} > \tau_r while H_r^{\mathrm{terminal}} \leq \tau_r.

Falsification: If the terminal state is a sufficient statistic for every modeled receptor consequence in the declared domain, the trajectory-level requirement is unnecessary for those mechanisms. This hypothesis is expected to fail for debris and overpressure mechanisms, which are already trajectory-based in operational tools; it is targeted at the post-capture transient gap.

H5 — Model-form robustness changes admissibility

Hypothesis: There exists a scenario in which a policy is admissible under one consequence model form but inadmissible under another, and the model-form uncertainty band is wider than the decision-reversal margin.

Falsification: If every policy’s admissibility is invariant across the declared model-form set, Tier-2 uncertainty does not affect the decision, and the single-model robust formulation is adequate for that domain.

H6 — Epistemic learning value

Hypothesis: For at least one policy excluded under the current \Theta_{\mathrm{param}}, there exists an investigation e such that \mathrm{VOR}_e > 0 and the policy becomes admissible under \mathcal{I}_e(\Theta_{\mathrm{param}}).

Falsification: If no investigation relaxes the effective constraint for any currently excluded policy, the epistemic-learning layer adds no decision value beyond static robustness.

H7 — Negative-control invariance

Hypothesis: In a scenario constructed so that the physical model predicts near-independence of H and A and the aggregate baseline is adequate, TARA does not produce a decision reversal.

Falsification: If TARA produces a decision reversal in the negative-control scenario, the framework is imposing a prior rather than detecting a physical separation, and its claimed diagnostic function is not supported.

