Chapter 4 — Literature and Regulatory Audit (with Pilot Findings)

4.1 Regulatory audit as the first extraction target

The v0.2 audit protocol (C1–C6) is retained, but the first documents scored are 14 CFR 450.101 and the NASA PRA Procedures Guide, not the academic literature. The pilot scoring is:

Document C1 Physical partition C2 H/A separation C3 Receptor granularity C4 Trajectory horizon C5 Joint dependence C6 Decision constraints
14 CFR 450.101 0.5 (capture not addressed; risk criteria are post-failure) 1 (collective, individual, aircraft, critical-asset criteria are separate) 1 (public, personnel, aircraft, critical assets) 0.5 (trajectory-based debris/toxicity, but not post-capture transient) 0 (criteria are separate, dependence not modeled) 1 (hard criteria, not weighted)
NASA PRA Procedures Guide 0.5 (LOC/LOV/LOM distinct end states) 1 (end states separate) 0.5 (crew, vehicle, mission; external receptors less developed) 0.5 (event trees, not trajectory functionals) 0 (end states treated as separate) 0.5 (requirements, but not chance-constrained optimization)

The pilot finding is that C2 is largely satisfied by the regulatory/PRA baseline. The genuine gaps are C5 (joint dependence), C4 for post-capture transients, and the decision-relevance question that none of the baseline documents formalizes.

4.2 Literature audit protocol (unchanged in structure, with independence requirement)

The PRISMA-guided protocol is retained. Two independent extractors score each document on C1–C6; inter-rater agreement is reported using Cohen’s \kappa. A score of 0.5 or 1 requires a page, section, or figure reference. The author does not self-score as the sole extractor.

4.3 Prior-art boundary (revised)

The following are explicitly acknowledged as prior art and are not claimed as contributions:

· separation of collective, individual, aircraft, and critical-asset risk (14 CFR 450.101);
· separation of LOC and LOV end states (NASA PRA);
· trajectory-based debris and overpressure risk (range-safety tools);
· chance-constrained and distributionally robust optimization (Wasserstein ambiguity sets);
· event trees, fault trees, copulas, Fréchet bounds, Pareto analysis.

The potential contribution is the integration: a mechanism-indexed partition, a joint dependence model with a granularity-deficiency test, a two-tier uncertainty architecture, and a state-feedback stopping-time decision layer with a regulatory mapping.

4.4 Pilot search status

A pilot search was conducted across IEEE Xplore, AIAA ARC, ScienceDirect, and Google Scholar using the Chapter 4 queries. The pilot confirms that distributionally robust chance-constrained optimization is mature and that simple \sup_{\theta} robustness is not competitive with Wasserstein-based formulations in the current literature. This motivates the Tier-1 CVaR reformulation in §2.8. The pilot also confirms that trajectory-level consequence modeling exists operationally (range-safety tools), which narrows the trajectory-horizon novelty claim to post-capture transients.

