Chapter 5 — Validation Methodology (Revised)

5.1 Baseline specification (new requirement)

Before any TARA experiment is run, three concrete baselines are implemented:

B1 — Regulatory aggregate. The E_c criterion of 14 CFR 450.101, computed over the same trajectory set, with the same physical model and the same receptor exposure data. This is the authoritative aggregate baseline, not a straw man.

B2 — PRA end-state tree. A NASA-style event tree with LOC/LOV/LOM end states, using the same initiating events and pivotal events as the TARA mechanism set. This tests whether the mechanism partition adds information beyond established PRA practice.

B3 — Scalarized expected loss. A single weighted objective combining safety and asset loss with declared weights. This is the decision-theoretic aggregate baseline.

TARA is compared against all three. A result that TARA differs from B3 but not from B1/B2 is not evidence of decision-relevance; it is evidence that the scalarization was poorly chosen.

5.2 Experiment set (revised)

Experiment Objective Falsification condition
E1 — Mechanism partition salience Test whether mechanism-indexed classes reduce within-class hazard spread relative to the v0.2 eight-class partition No reduction in spread across the scenario set
E2 — Joint dependence estimation Estimate P(H,A) and compare with product of marginals; report dependence-sensitive risk intervals Dependence statistically indistinguishable from independence for all receptor–asset pairs
E3 — Trajectory vs. terminal for post-capture transients Compare H_r^{\mathrm{trajectory}} with H_r^{\mathrm{terminal}} for thermal, safing, and stabilization mechanisms Terminal state is sufficient for every modeled receptor
E4 — Decision-reversal margin Compute \Delta_{\mathrm{rev}} against B1, B2, B3; compare with Tier-1 + Tier-2 uncertainty band \Delta_{\mathrm{rev}} exceeds the uncertainty band in every scenario
E5 — Model-form robustness Evaluate policy admissibility across \mathcal{M}_{\mathrm{form}}; report model-form infeasibility where it occurs Admissibility invariant across all model forms
E6 — Negative control Construct a near-independence scenario; verify that TARA does not reverse the decision TARA reverses the decision in the negative control
E7 — Epistemic learning value Compute \mathrm{VOR}_e for candidate investigations; identify policies that become admissible No investigation relaxes the effective constraint

5.3 Statistical reporting

The rule of three (Clopper–Pearson one-sided 95% upper bound p_{\mathrm{upper}} \approx 3/N) is cited as such and is applied only to independent Bernoulli sampling. For importance sampling, subset simulation, or correlated batches, the estimator-specific confidence procedure and variance diagnostic are reported. No probability claim is made from zero-failure samples alone.

Power analysis is reported for E4: the Monte Carlo sample size must be sufficient to detect \Delta_{\mathrm{rev}} at the declared confidence level. If the required N is computationally infeasible, E4 is reported as underpowered, not as a negative result.

