Chapter 6 — Case-Study Protocol: Starship Flight-Test Record (New)

6.1 Purpose

The Starship flight-test record provides a publicly documented sequence of recovery attempts that maps onto the TARA mechanism set and directly tests the decision-relevance hypothesis without requiring new physical simulation. The case study is descriptive and classificatory, not a validation of the physical models.

6.2 Documented cases

Case A — Flight 7 (January 2025): booster catch success, ship loss, external aviation consequences. The Super Heavy booster was successfully caught by the tower arms, while the upper stage disintegrated, debris fell near Turks and Caicos, and flights altered course. Under a single-vehicle trajectory framework, the booster catch is \Omega_{m_1} (no hazard) and the ship loss is outside the trajectory space. The case demonstrates that capture success in one element can coexist with external hazard from another element, and it forces TARA to declare whether multi-element missions are in scope. Version 0.3 scopes the core framework to a single recovery trajectory and treats multi-element coupling as an extension requirement (§6.4).

Case B — Offshore divert (Flight 6 and subsequent). The booster was diverted to an offshore splashdown and exploded in the Gulf of Mexico, with the launch operator prioritizing established safety criteria over the experimental recovery objective. This is mechanism m_7: vehicle loss without external receptor hazard. It is the operational realization of the v0.2 H3 scenario, but it also shows that the decision was made by a real-time stopping rule, not a batch optimization—supporting the §2.9 formulation.

Case C — Flight 12 mishap and investigation closure (May–July 2026). The FAA required a mishap investigation after a booster return failure; the investigation identified corrective actions, and the FAA closed the review in July 2026, permitting Flight 13. This is the epistemic-learning loop: the uncertainty set \Theta_{\mathrm{param}} was reduced by investigation evidence, and the effective constraint was relaxed. It motivates the \mathrm{VOR}_e operator (§2.11) and H6.

6.3 Classification exercise

The documented cases are classified into the mechanism set as a pilot residual audit. The exercise is expected to expose boundary problems: Case A requires a multi-element extension; Case B is a clean m_7; Case C is an epistemic operator, not a trajectory class. The classification table is reported as a descriptive annex, not as a hazard probability estimate.

6.4 Multi-element extension requirement

If TARA is to cover integrated missions (booster + ship), the trajectory space must be extended to a product \mathcal{W} = \mathcal{W}_{\mathrm{booster}} \times \mathcal{W}_{\mathrm{ship}}, and the hazard functional must include cross-element dependencies (shared airspace, sequential operations, debris from one element affecting the other). Version 0.3 declares this an open extension, not a solved problem. The single-element framework is a deliberate scope limitation, not an implicit claim of completeness.

