Chapter 7 — Assumptions, Limitations, and Research Integrity

The assumptions and limitations of v0.2 are retained, with the following revisions.

7.1 Model-form uncertainty is first-class. The framework no longer treats \Theta as a single set. Tier-2 model-form uncertainty is evaluated as a model set, and model-form infeasibility is reported as a finding, not averaged away.

7.2 Classification ambiguity is represented, not eliminated. The mechanism map \mu is a set-valued map under epistemic uncertainty. The hazard probability is reported as an interval when \mu(\omega) is ambiguous. This is more honest than a deterministic assignment rule.

7.3 The regulatory baseline is authoritative. TARA does not claim that 14 CFR 450 or NASA PRA is incorrect. It claims that those frameworks do not formalize the joint dependence between hazard and asset outcomes at the trajectory level, and it tests whether that omission is decision-relevant.

7.4 No universal normalization. Receptor-specific physical and consequence measures are retained. Where a scalar is required for Pareto reporting, the transformation \psi_r is declared and justified. Mandatory safety limits are not assigned compensating economic values.

7.5 Reproducibility. All classification rules, mechanism-dominance rules, \Theta_{\mathrm{param}} constructions, model-form sets, and statistical procedures are documented. Any future numerical result must be reproducible from the documented inputs.

7.6 Pilot findings are preliminary. The regulatory scoring in §4.1 and the case classifications in §6.3 are pilot exercises, not validated audits. They are reported to demonstrate the audit method and to motivate the revised hypotheses, not to establish final conclusions.

