Chapter 8 — Expected Contributions (Repositioned)

The contributions of v0.2 are revised in light of the regulatory baseline audit.

Contribution 1 (narrowed). A mechanism-indexed physical partition with a formal granularity-deficiency test, replacing the product-space enumeration. The contribution is the mechanism-based classification and the test of whether it reduces within-class hazard heterogeneity, not the bare act of separating capture from safety.

Contribution 2 (repositioned). A joint dependence model with a decision-reversal margin \Delta_{\mathrm{rev}}, compared against three specified baselines: the 14 CFR 450 E_c aggregate, the NASA PRA end-state tree, and a scalarized expected-loss model. The contribution is the quantitative decision-relevance criterion, not the claim that separation is novel.

Contribution 3 (new emphasis). A two-tier uncertainty architecture distinguishing parametric epistemic uncertainty (Tier 1, CVaR or supremum) from model-form uncertainty (Tier 2, model-set admissibility), with explicit reporting of model-form infeasibility.

Contribution 4 (operationalized). A state-feedback stopping-time decision layer with distributionally robust receptor constraints and a lexicographic fallback for infeasible cases. This connects the batch assessment to the real-time abort decision, addressing the runtime-assurance gap in v0.2.

Contribution 5 (new). An epistemic-learning value operator \mathrm{VOR}_e for pre-posterior analysis of mishap investigations, motivated by the Starship Flight 12 investigation sequence.

Contribution 6 (methodological). A falsification protocol with a negative control (H7) and a power-analysis requirement for decision-reversal detection. A negative result—separation is not decision-relevant under specified conditions—is treated as a legitimate outcome.

The contribution is considered established only to the extent supported by analytical results, baseline comparisons, and appropriately validated computational evidence. No claim of practical advantage is made in the absence of E4/E5 results.

---

Chapter 9 — Conclusion

TARA v0.3 is a revision of v0.2 in response to the regulatory baseline audit and to the methodological criticisms of the original formulation. The core distinction—between receptor-specific hazard consequences and asset-recovery outcomes—is retained, but its novelty is repositioned: the regulatory and PRA literature already separates these dimensions at the outcome level. The open questions are the joint dependence, the granularity of the physical partition, the model-form uncertainty, and the real-time decision interface.

The framework now has a sharper falsifiable core:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Mechanism-indexed trajectory} \\
&\longrightarrow \begin{cases}
\text{Receptor-specific } H_r \text{ with horizon flag} \\
\text{Asset state } A \text{ with operational downtime}
\end{cases} \\
&\longrightarrow \text{Joint law } P^{H,A} \\
&\longrightarrow \Delta_{\mathrm{rev}} \text{ vs. uncertainty band} \\
&\longrightarrow \begin{cases}
\text{Decision-relevant separation} \\
\text{or} \\
\text{Aggregate decision adequate}
\end{cases}
\end{aligned}
}

The central proposition remains:

\text{Mechanical capture outcome} \not\equiv \text{Safety outcome}

but the scientific question is no longer whether this inequality can be stated. It is whether the inequality matters enough to change a decision, under a declared uncertainty architecture, against a specified regulatory baseline. TARA v0.3 is designed to answer that question, and to report a negative answer honestly if the evidence requires it.

