Appendix B — Measure-Theoretic Foundations

B.1 Regularity assumptions on the physical model

The measurability results below depend on the physical model having regular outcome functionals. These assumptions are stated explicitly.

Assumption B.1 (Trajectory space). \mathcal{W} = C(\mathcal{I}; \mathbb{R}^n) equipped with the supremum norm \| \omega \|_\infty = \sup_{t \in \mathcal{I}} |X(t)| is a separable Banach space, hence a Polish space, hence a standard Borel space when equipped with the Borel \sigma-algebra \mathcal{F}.

Assumption B.2 (Continuity of capture-status functionals). Each of S_{\mathrm{cap}}, S_{\mathrm{veh}}, S_{\mathrm{fac}} is determined by a finite family of continuous real-valued functionals \{g_i : \mathcal{W} \to \mathbb{R}\} and a finite family of threshold pairs \{(\ell_i, u_i)\} such that S = s iff g_i(\omega) \in [\ell_i, u_i] for all i in the index set of s. This covers the typical operational definitions: contact force above threshold for duration, stabilization interval length, etc.

Assumption B.3 (Continuity of hazard descriptors). Each z_{r,j}(t, \cdot) : \mathcal{W} \to \mathbb{R} is continuous in the supremum norm, uniformly in t \in \mathcal{I}. This implies that both Z^{\max}_{r,j}(\omega) = \sup_t z_{r,j}(t,\omega) and Z^{\mathrm{cum}}_{r,j}(\omega) = \int_\mathcal{I} \phi_{r,j}(z_{r,j}(t,\omega)) dt are continuous in \omega, provided \phi_{r,j} is continuous and bounded on the range of z_{r,j}.

B.2 Proposition B.1 (Measurability of the physical outcome map)

Statement. Under Assumption B.2, \mathcal{E} : \mathcal{W} \to \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{cap}} \times \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{veh}} \times \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{fac}} is Borel-measurable.

Proof. Since the codomain is finite, it suffices to show \mathcal{E}^{-1}(\{(s_c, s_v, s_f)\}) \in \mathcal{F} for each triple (s_c, s_v, s_f). Let I_c, I_v, I_f be the finite index sets defining the three components under Assumption B.2, and let \{(g_i, \ell_i, u_i)\}_{i \in I_c \cup I_v \cup I_f} be the corresponding continuous functionals and threshold intervals. Then

\mathcal{E}^{-1}(\{(s_c, s_v, s_f)\}) = \bigcap_{i \in I_c \cup I_v \cup I_f} g_i^{-1}([\ell_i, u_i]).

Each g_i^{-1}([\ell_i, u_i]) is closed (preimage of a closed set under a continuous map), hence Borel. A finite intersection of Borel sets is Borel. ∎

Remark B.1. If any component of S_{\mathrm{cap}}, S_{\mathrm{veh}}, S_{\mathrm{fac}} is defined by an open condition (e.g., “contact force exceeds threshold at some instant”) that is not the complement of a closed condition, measurability may still hold if the functional is Borel, but not necessarily continuous. The proof only requires Borel-measurability of each g_i, and continuity is a sufficient condition. The assumption should therefore be read as “Borel-measurable outcome functionals,” with continuity as the operational sufficient condition.

B.3 Proposition B.2 (Measurability of the mechanism-indexed partition)

Statement. Under Assumption B.3, the set-valued mechanism map \tilde{\mu} : \mathcal{W} \to 2^{\mathcal{M}} \setminus \{\emptyset\} is Borel-measurable (i.e., \tilde{\mu}^{-1}(\{A \subseteq \mathcal{M} : A \cap F \neq \emptyset\}) \in \mathcal{F} for every finite F \subseteq \mathcal{M}). Consequently, for each m_j \in \mathcal{M}, the set \tilde{\Omega}_{m_j} = \{\omega : m_j \in \tilde{\mu}(\omega)\} is Borel.

Proof. Each m_j \in \tilde{\mu}(\omega) iff some hazard descriptor associated with mechanism m_j exceeds the dominance comparison threshold relative to other mechanisms. Define d_j(\omega) =  “mechanism m_j is dominant or co-dominant at \omega” as a logical combination of comparisons of continuous functionals. Under Assumption B.3, each comparison is Borel-measurable, and finite logical combinations preserve Borel measurability. Therefore \tilde{\Omega}_{m_j} = \{\omega : d_j(\omega) = 1\} is Borel. ∎

Remark B.2. The specific dominance rule matters. If dominance is defined as “the mechanism contributing the largest hazard descriptor,” and two mechanisms tie, \tilde{\mu}(\omega) contains both. The set \tilde{\Omega}_{m_j} is then the preimage of a comparison inequality, which is Borel if the descriptors are Borel. The exhaustiveness of the set-valued map \bigcup_j \tilde{\Omega}_{m_j} = \mathcal{W} is automatic since \tilde{\mu}(\omega) is nonempty by the dominance rule (at least one mechanism achieves the maximum).

B.4 Proposition B.3 (Pushforward measure well-definedness)

Statement. For any policy D and any measurable event E \in \mathcal{F}, the measure P_D : \mathcal{F} \to [0,1] exists (as a probability measure), and the pushforward P_D^{H,A}(B \times C) = P_D((H,A)^{-1}(B \times C)) is a well-defined probability measure on (\mathcal{H} \times \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{H}) \otimes \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{A})).

Proof. The existence of P_D on (\mathcal{W},\mathcal{F}) is by assumption (it is the law of the trajectory under the physical model and the policy). The map (H,A) : \mathcal{W} \to \mathcal{H} \times \mathcal{A} is Borel-measurable by Propositions B.1 and the Borel-measurability of H_r and A (assumed in Assumption B.3 and stated in §2.5/§2.6). The standard pushforward measure theorem (e.g., Kallenberg, Foundations of Modern Probability, Theorem 1.8) then guarantees that P_D^{H,A} is a well-defined probability measure on the product \sigma-algebra. ∎

B.5 Proposition B.4 (Measurability of the closed-loop measure)

Statement. Under Assumptions B.1–B.3 and the measurability of the state-feedback action map \pi and stopping rule \tau, the closed-loop trajectory distribution P_{(\pi,\tau)} is well-defined on (\mathcal{W}, \mathcal{F}).

Proof. The closed-loop dynamics X_{t+1} = f(X_t, \pi(X_t), \xi_{t+1}) define a Markov transition kernel K : \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{X}) \to [0,1], measurable in the first argument under measurability of f and \pi. The stopping rule \tau defines a stopping time with respect to the natural filtration. The Ionescu–Tulcea theorem guarantees the existence of a unique probability measure on the path space \mathcal{X}^{\mathbb{N}} consistent with the kernel and the initial distribution. Mapping this path measure to \mathcal{W} = C(\mathcal{I};\mathbb{R}^n) via the trajectory-extraction functional yields P_{(\pi,\tau)}, provided the extraction is Borel-measurable (which it is, since \mathcal{W} is a Polish space and the trajectory is a continuous function of the discrete-time path under the interpolation convention). ∎

Remark B.3 (Honest limitation). If the state space is unbounded or the action set is continuous without compactness, the Ionescu–Tulcea construction still yields a measure, but the expected loss \mathbb{E}[L_A] may be infinite. The decision problem in §2.9 must therefore be restricted to policies for which \mathbb{E}[L_A] < \infty, or the loss function L_A must be truncated at a declared operational ceiling. This is an assumption, not a theorem.

