Appendix C — Mechanism-Indexed Partition: Formal Proofs

C.1 Proposition C.1 (Deterministic exhaustiveness and mutual exclusivity)

Statement. Under the deterministic mechanism map \mu : \mathcal{W} \to \mathcal{M}, the partition \{\Omega_{m_j}\}_{j=1}^M with \Omega_{m_j} = \mu^{-1}(\{m_j\}) satisfies \bigcup_j \Omega_{m_j} = \mathcal{W} and \Omega_{m_i} \cap \Omega_{m_j} = \emptyset for i \neq j.

Proof. Exhaustiveness: for any \omega \in \mathcal{W}, \mu(\omega) \in \mathcal{M} by definition, so \omega \in \mu^{-1}(\{\mu(\omega)\}) = \Omega_{\mu(\omega)} \subseteq \bigcup_j \Omega_{m_j}. Mutual exclusivity: if \omega \in \Omega_{m_i} \cap \Omega_{m_j} with i \neq j, then \mu(\omega) = m_i and \mu(\omega) = m_j, contradicting m_i \neq m_j. ∎

C.2 Proposition C.2 (Set-valued exhaustiveness under ambiguity)

Statement. Under the set-valued mechanism map \tilde{\mu} : \mathcal{W} \to 2^{\mathcal{M}} \setminus \{\emptyset\}, the sets \tilde{\Omega}_{m_j} = \{\omega : m_j \in \tilde{\mu}(\omega)\} satisfy \bigcup_j \tilde{\Omega}_{m_j} = \mathcal{W}. They are not in general mutually exclusive.

Proof. Exhaustiveness: for any \omega, \tilde{\mu}(\omega) \neq \emptyset, so it contains some m_j, hence \omega \in \tilde{\Omega}_{m_j}. Non-exclusivity: if \tilde{\mu}(\omega) = \{m_i, m_j\} with i \neq j, then \omega \in \tilde{\Omega}_{m_i} \cap \tilde{\Omega}_{m_j}. ∎

C.3 Proposition C.3 (Ambiguity interval for hazard probability)

Statement. For each receptor r, the hazard probability under set-valued mechanism assignment satisfies

\max_j \tilde{P}_r(\tilde{\Omega}_{m_j}) \leq P_D(B_r) \leq \min\left(1, \sum_j \tilde{P}_r(\tilde{\Omega}_{m_j})\right)

where \tilde{P}_r(\tilde{\Omega}_{m_j}) = P_D(B_r \cap \tilde{\Omega}_{m_j}) is the partial hazard probability restricted to \tilde{\Omega}_{m_j}.

Proof. Lower bound: B_r \cap \tilde{\Omega}_{m_j} \subseteq B_r for each j, so P_D(B_r \cap \tilde{\Omega}_{m_j}) \leq P_D(B_r). Upper bound: B_r = B_r \cap \mathcal{W} = B_r \cap (\bigcup_j \tilde{\Omega}_{m_j}) = \bigcup_j (B_r \cap \tilde{\Omega}_{m_j}), and by subadditivity, P_D(B_r) \leq \sum_j P_D(B_r \cap \tilde{\Omega}_{m_j}). The bound \leq 1 is trivial. ∎

Remark C.1. The interval collapses to a point when the mechanism assignment is deterministic (the case of Proposition C.1). Under ambiguity, the interval is the honest representation of what the classification procedure can infer from the physical model.

C.4 Proposition C.4 (Granularity-deficiency comparison)

Statement. Define the granularity-deficiency measure for receptor r under partition \Pi by

\eta_r(\Pi) = \mathbb{E}_\omega\left[\mathrm{Var}_{\omega' \sim P_D(\cdot | \Pi(\omega))}(\mathbf{1}_{B_r}(\omega'))\right]

where \Pi(\omega) denotes the cell of the partition containing \omega. If partition \Pi_1 refines partition \Pi_2 (each cell of \Pi_1 is a subset of a cell of \Pi_2), then \eta_r(\Pi_1) \leq \eta_r(\Pi_2).

Proof. Let X = \mathbf{1}_{B_r} and let \mathcal{G}_1 = \sigma(\Pi_1), \mathcal{G}_2 = \sigma(\Pi_2) be the \sigma-algebras generated by the two partitions. Refinement means \mathcal{G}_1 \supseteq \mathcal{G}_2. By the law of total variance, \mathrm{Var}(X) = \mathbb{E}[\mathrm{Var}(X \mid \mathcal{G}_i)] + \mathrm{Var}(\mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}_i]) for i=1,2. Subtracting gives

\eta_r(\Pi_1) - \eta_r(\Pi_2) = \mathrm{Var}(\mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}_2]) - \mathrm{Var}(\mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}_1]).

Since \mathcal{G}_1 \supseteq \mathcal{G}_2, \mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}_1] = \mathbb{E}[\mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}_1] \mid \mathcal{G}_2], so \mathrm{Var}(\mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}_1]) \geq \mathrm{Var}(\mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}_2]) (variance cannot decrease by further conditioning). Therefore \eta_r(\Pi_1) \leq \eta_r(\Pi_2). ∎

Remark C.2 (Honest caveat). The mechanism partition and the v0.2 eight-class partition are not, in general, in a refinement relation. The mechanism partition classifies by hazard-generating process; the v0.2 partition classifies by terminal integrity state. Neither is a refinement of the other. Proposition C.4 therefore provides a sufficient condition for granularity improvement, not a general proof that mechanism-indexing improves granularity. Hypothesis H1 must be tested empirically in E1, not derived from Proposition C.4.

