Appendix D — Two-Tier Uncertainty: Proofs and Properties

D.1 Proposition D.1 (CVaR is a coherent risk measure)

Statement. For a random variable X on a probability space, the Conditional Value-at-Risk at level \beta \in (0,1) defined by

\mathrm{CVaR}_\beta(X) = \min_{t \in \mathbb{R}} \left\{ t + \frac{1}{1-\beta} \mathbb{E}[(X - t)^+] \right\}

satisfies monotonicity, positive homogeneity, translation equivariance, and subadditivity. It is therefore a coherent risk measure in the sense of Artzner et al. (1999).

Proof. Rockafellar and Uryasev (2000, Optimization of Conditional Value-at-Risk) establish the representation and its equivalence to the tail-mean \mathbb{E}[X \mid X \geq \mathrm{VaR}_\beta(X)] for continuous distributions. The four coherence properties are established in Artzner et al. (1999) and Acerbi and Tasche (2002). We do not reprove them here; the reference is standard. ∎

D.2 Proposition D.2 (CVaR dominates the point supremum only in the limit)

Statement. For a finite parameter set \Theta_{\mathrm{param}} = \{\theta_1,\ldots,\theta_n\} with equal weights 1/n, and for any \beta \in (0,1),

\mathrm{CVaR}_\beta\left(P_{\theta}(B_r) : \theta \in \Theta_{\mathrm{param}}\right) \leq \sup_{\theta \in \Theta_{\mathrm{param}}} P_{\theta}(B_r).

Equality holds in the limit \beta \to 1.

Proof. For discrete uniform distributions on n points, \mathrm{CVaR}_\beta is a weighted average of the largest (1-\beta)n values (with fractional weight on the boundary). As \beta \to 1, the weight concentrates on the largest value, giving the supremum. Since any weighted average of a set of values is at most the maximum, the inequality holds for all \beta. ∎

Corollary D.2.1. The CVaR constraint \mathrm{CVaR}_\beta(P_\theta(B_r)) \leq \alpha_r is less conservative than the supremum constraint \sup_\theta P_\theta(B_r) \leq \alpha_r for any \beta < 1. The degree of relaxation is controlled by \beta.

Remark D.1. The main-text §2.8 retains the supremum constraint for the case where only a set (not a distribution) over \Theta_{\mathrm{param}} is defensible, and uses CVaR only when a distribution is justified by Bayesian calibration. This is the correct epistemic stance: CVaR requires a probability measure on \Theta_{\mathrm{param}}, which is itself an epistemic commitment.

D.3 Proposition D.3 (Model-form admissibility is well-defined)

Statement. Under the Tier-2 model set \mathcal{M}_{\mathrm{form}} = \{\Phi_r^{(1)},\ldots,\Phi_r^{(L)}\}, define

\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{adm}}^{\mathrm{form}} = \left\{ (\pi,\tau) : P_{(\pi,\tau)}^{(l)}(B_r) \leq \alpha_r \ \forall r \in \mathcal{R}, \ \forall l \in \{1,\ldots,L\} \right\}.

Then \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{adm}}^{\mathrm{form}} is a well-defined subset of the policy space, and it is contained in the admissible set \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{adm}}^{(l)} for each model form l:

\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{adm}}^{\mathrm{form}} = \bigcap_{l=1}^L \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{adm}}^{(l)}.

Proof. Immediate from the definition: a policy is in the intersection iff it satisfies the constraint under every model form iff it is in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{adm}}^{\mathrm{form}}. ∎

D.4 Proposition D.4 (Model-form infeasibility cannot be averaged away)

Statement. If \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{adm}}^{\mathrm{form}} = \emptyset but \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{adm}}^{(l)} \neq \emptyset for each l, then no averaging over model forms can produce a policy that is admissible under every model form. Specifically, any convex combination of policy-valued distributions Q = \sum_l w_l Q_l with Q_l \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{adm}}^{(l)} and \sum_l w_l = 1, w_l > 0, may fail admissibility under some model form even if each Q_l is admissible under its own model form.

Proof. By construction, Q_l is admissible under \Phi^{(l)}, but it may be inadmissible under \Phi^{(l')} for l' \neq l (that is the meaning of \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{adm}}^{\mathrm{form}} = \emptyset). If w_l > 0 and \Phi^{(l')} assigns a constraint violation to Q_l, then the mixture Q inherits positive probability of violation under \Phi^{(l')}. Model averaging therefore does not restore model-robust admissibility. The claim that it does is a common error. ∎

Corollary D.4.1. When \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{adm}}^{\mathrm{form}} = \emptyset, the framework must report model-form infeasibility and, per §2.9, apply the lexicographic fallback. It must not select a preferred model form to manufacture feasibility.

D.5 Remark on when CVaR and supremum coincide

For finite \Theta_{\mathrm{param}} with equal weights, CVaR_\beta coincides with the supremum only at \beta = 1. For \beta < 1, CVaR strictly dominates the tail mean and lies strictly below the supremum unless all tail values coincide. The choice of \beta is therefore a declared research input, and its effect on the admissible set must be reported (sensitivity analysis) rather than fixed silently.

