Appendix E — Decision Layer: Well-Posedness and Existence

E.1 Proposition E.1 (Closed-loop measure well-posedness)

Reproduced from B.5 for completeness.

Statement. Under Assumptions B.1–B.3, measurability of \pi and \tau, and finite \mathbb{E}[L_A] for the class of admissible policies, the closed-loop trajectory measure P_{(\pi,\tau)} is well-defined on (\mathcal{W}, \mathcal{F}).

Proof. See Proposition B.4. The additional assumption \mathbb{E}[L_A] < \infty is required for the objective functional to be finite; it does not affect measurability. ∎

E.2 Proposition E.2 (Lexicographic fallback existence)

Statement. Let the receptor priority order be r_1 \succ r_2 \succ \cdots \succ r_m. For each action a \in \mathcal{A} (finite), define the violation vector

v(a) = \left( (P_{\pi_a}(B_{r_1}) - \alpha_{r_1})^+,\, \ldots,\, (P_{\pi_a}(B_{r_m}) - \alpha_{r_m})^+ \right) \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^m

where x^+ = \max(x, 0). Define the lexicographic order on \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^m: v \prec_{\mathrm{lex}} v' iff v_j < v'_j at the smallest index j where they differ. Then the set \{v(a) : a \in \mathcal{A}\} has a lexicographic minimum, and the corresponding action(s) constitute the fallback recommendation.

Proof. \mathcal{A} is finite by assumption, so \{v(a) : a \in \mathcal{A}\} is finite. Any finite subset of \mathbb{R}^m has a lexicographic minimum, since lexicographic order on \mathbb{R}^m is a total order and finite sets are well-ordered under any total order. The minimal action is the fallback. If multiple actions share the minimal violation vector, they are tied on the priority-ordered constraints, and the framework selects among them by secondary criteria (e.g., expected asset loss). ∎

Remark E.1. The lexicographic fallback is a policy recommendation, not a certification. If the minimal violation vector is strictly positive (i.e., no action satisfies all constraints), the fallback identifies the least-violating action under the declared priority order, and this must be reported as an infeasibility outcome—not as an admissible decision.

E.3 Proposition E.3 (Decision-reversal margin existence)

Statement. Let \mathcal{D} be a finite policy set, and let g^{\mathrm{agg}}(\alpha) = \mathrm{rank}^{\mathrm{agg}} and g^{\mathrm{TARA}}(\alpha) = \mathrm{rank}^{\mathrm{TARA}} denote the policy rankings under the aggregate and two-axis assessments respectively, as functions of the constraint level \alpha = (\alpha_r)_{r \in \mathcal{R}}. Define

\Delta_{\mathrm{rev}}(\alpha) = \min\left\{ \delta > 0 : g^{\mathrm{agg}}(\alpha') \neq g^{\mathrm{TARA}}(\alpha'') \text{ for some } \|\alpha' - \alpha\| = \delta \right\}.

Under either of the following conditions, \Delta_{\mathrm{rev}} exists (is finite or +\infty):

(i) the maps \alpha \mapsto P_{\theta}(B_r) and \alpha \mapsto L_A(\pi,\tau) are continuous, and the rankings are continuous in the loss values; or

(ii) the policy set is finite and the rankings are piecewise constant in \alpha (typical for discrete decision problems).

Proof. Case (ii): the rankings take finitely many values on the finite policy set, and as \alpha varies over a bounded domain, the transition set \{ \alpha : \mathrm{rank}^{\mathrm{agg}}(\alpha) \neq \mathrm{rank}^{\mathrm{TARA}}(\alpha) \} is a finite union of intervals (or their boundaries). The minimum of the width over this set exists if the set is nonempty; otherwise \Delta_{\mathrm{rev}} = +\infty. Case (i): by continuity, the transition set is open, and its boundary is closed; the minimum over the closure exists by compactness of the bounded domain. ∎

Remark E.2. Under discontinuity (finite policy sets with non-smooth loss landscape), \Delta_{\mathrm{rev}} is a finite minimum over the discrete set of transition points, not a limit. This is the typical case for RLV recovery decisions, since capture is a discrete event and abort actions are discrete commands.

