Appendix F — Statistical Guarantees

F.1 Proposition F.1 (Rule of three)

Statement. Let N independent Bernoulli trials with common success probability p produce zero successes. The one-sided (1-\gamma) Clopper–Pearson upper confidence bound for p satisfies

(1 - p_u)^N = \gamma,

hence p_u = 1 - \gamma^{1/N}. For large N and \gamma = 0.05, p_u \approx 3/N.

Proof. The likelihood of zero successes is (1-p)^N, which is strictly decreasing in p. The Clopper–Pearson upper bound is the value p_u satisfying \Pr_{p = p_u}(\text{zero successes}) = \gamma, i.e., (1-p_u)^N = \gamma. Solving gives p_u = 1 - \gamma^{1/N}. For the asymptotic approximation: \gamma^{1/N} = e^{\ln\gamma / N} \approx 1 + \ln\gamma / N = 1 - 3.0/N for \gamma = 0.05 (since |\ln 0.05| = 2.996 \approx 3). Therefore p_u \approx 3/N. ∎

F.2 Proposition F.2 (Clopper–Pearson bound is exact and conservative)

Statement. The Clopper–Pearson upper bound p_u = 1 - \gamma^{1/N} is exact in the sense that \Pr_{p}(p \leq p_u) \geq 1-\gamma for all p \in [0,1], with equality at p = p_u. It is conservative relative to the asymptotic normal approximation for small N.

Proof. Standard; see Clopper and Pearson (1934) and Brown, Cai, and DasGupta (2001, Statistical Science). The exactness follows from the likelihood ratio monotonicity. ∎

F.3 Proposition F.3 (Sample size for decision-reversal detection in E4)

Statement. Suppose the decision-reversal experiment has null probability p_0 = 0 (no reversal) and alternative p_1 = \delta_{\mathrm{rev}} (reversal probability under the two-axis assessment). The Monte Carlo sample size required to detect the reversal with one-sided significance \alpha and power 1-\beta satisfies

N \geq \frac{(z_{1-\alpha} \sqrt{p_0(1-p_0)} + z_{1-\beta} \sqrt{p_1(1-p_1)})^2}{(p_1 - p_0)^2}

in the standard normal-approximation calculation, with the adjustment that for p_0 = 0, the approximation requires N p_1 \gtrsim 5 for the normal approximation to be accurate.

Proof. Standard two-sample (or one-sample against a null) proportion test sample-size formula. The numerator is the sum of the critical values times the null and alternative standard errors, and the denominator is the squared effect size. For p_0 = 0 and small p_1, the exact Clopper–Pearson requirement may be more stringent; the normal formula is a lower bound. ∎

Remark F.1. If the required N exceeds the computational budget of the physical simulation, E4 is reported as underpowered. It is not reported as a negative result.

F.4 Corollary F.4 (Estimator misspecification risk)

Statement. If the sampler is importance-weighted, subset-simulation based, or correlated, the bound p_u = 1 - \gamma^{1/N} does not apply and may underestimate the true upper bound.

Proof. The Clopper–Pearson derivation assumes independent identically distributed Bernoulli trials. Importance sampling introduces weighted trials with effective sample size N_{\mathrm{eff}} < N; subset simulation produces correlated samples with effective information less than N; Markov-chain samples are autocorrelated. In each case, the effective number of independent observations is smaller, and the bound 3/N underestimates the true uncertainty. The correct estimator-specific confidence procedure must be used (e.g., effective sample size for importance sampling, batch-means variance for MCMC). ∎

