Appendix G — Horizon-Truncation

G.1 Proposition G.1 (Monotonicity of cumulative-exposure hazard under horizon extension)

Statement. For cumulative-exposure hazard descriptors Z^{\mathrm{cum}}_{r,j}(\omega) = \int_{t_0}^{T+\Delta} \phi_{r,j}(z_{r,j}(t,\omega)) dt with \phi_{r,j} \geq 0, the event B_r^{\mathrm{cum},\Delta} = \{ \omega : H_r^{\mathrm{cum}}(\omega; \Delta) > \tau_r \} is monotone non-decreasing in \Delta:

\Delta_1 \leq \Delta_2 \implies B_r^{\mathrm{cum},\Delta_1} \subseteq B_r^{\mathrm{cum},\Delta_2}.

Proof. For \Delta_1 \leq \Delta_2 and \phi_{r,j} \geq 0, the integral over [t_0, T+\Delta_1] is at most the integral over [t_0, T+\Delta_2]. Hence H_r^{\mathrm{cum}}(\omega; \Delta_1) \leq H_r^{\mathrm{cum}}(\omega; \Delta_2). If H_r^{\mathrm{cum}}(\omega; \Delta_1) > \tau_r, then H_r^{\mathrm{cum}}(\omega; \Delta_2) > \tau_r, so \omega \in B_r^{\mathrm{cum},\Delta_2}. ∎

Remark G.1. Monotonicity of the event does not imply monotonicity of the probability. P(B_r^{\mathrm{cum},\Delta}) is monotone non-decreasing in \Delta by monotone convergence, but it converges to a limit bounded above by 1. The horizon-truncation functional \delta_r(\Delta) = P(B_r^{\mathrm{cum},\Delta}) - P(B_r^{\mathrm{cum},\Delta_{\mathrm{ref}}}) is therefore non-negative and bounded, and converges to some limit \delta_r(\infty) \in [0,1]. The question is whether this limit is reached before the maximum computationally feasible \Delta.

G.2 Proposition G.2 (Non-monotonicity of peak-exposure hazard)

Statement. For peak-exposure hazard descriptors Z^{\max}_{r,j}(\omega) = \sup_{t \in [t_0, T+\Delta]} z_{r,j}(t,\omega), the event B_r^{\max,\Delta} = \{ \omega : H_r^{\max}(\omega; \Delta) > \tau_r \} is monotone non-decreasing in \Delta, but the increments \delta_r^{\max}(\Delta) = P(B_r^{\max,\Delta}) - P(B_r^{\max,\Delta - \delta}) are not necessarily monotone in \Delta.

Proof. Monotonicity of the event follows from \sup over a larger interval being at least the \sup over a smaller interval. Non-monotonicity of increments: the peak may be captured entirely in [t_0, T+\Delta_0] for some \Delta_0, in which case B_r^{\max,\Delta} = B_r^{\max,\Delta_0} for all \Delta \geq \Delta_0, and increments are zero. But if a delayed mechanism produces a new peak at some \Delta_1 > \Delta_0, increments are positive at \Delta_1 and zero elsewhere. The increments therefore may oscillate, and the horizon-truncation flag cannot rely on monotone convergence of increments. ∎

G.3 Proposition G.3 (Flag well-posedness)

Statement. Define the horizon-truncation flag by

\mathrm{flag}_r = \begin{cases}
\text{un-truncated} & \text{if } |\delta_r(\Delta_{\max}) - \delta_r(\Delta_{\max} - \delta_{\mathrm{step}})| \leq \varepsilon \\
\text{truncated} & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}

for a declared tolerance \varepsilon and step \delta_{\mathrm{step}}. Then the flag is well-defined: it depends only on declared quantities and computed values of \delta_r.

Proof. Immediate: the flag is a deterministic function of the declared \varepsilon, \delta_{\mathrm{step}}, \Delta_{\max}, and the computed values of \delta_r at the two horizons. No undetermined quantities appear. ∎

Remark G.2. The flag does not establish that delayed consequences beyond \Delta_{\max} are absent; it only establishes that no further truncation-detectable growth is observed between \Delta_{\max} - \delta_{\mathrm{step}} and \Delta_{\max}. A delayed mechanism with a very long incubation period would not be detected. This is an inherent limitation of finite-horizon assessment, not a flaw in the flag; it is reported as a caveat alongside the flag.

