Appendix I — Fréchet–Hoeffding Bounds and Dependence

I.1 Proposition I.1 (Fréchet–Hoeffding bounds)

Statement. For any two events C, D \in \mathcal{F} with probabilities p_C = P(C) and p_D = P(D),

\max(0, p_C + p_D - 1) \leq P(C \cap D) \leq \min(p_C, p_D).

Proof. Upper bound: C \cap D \subseteq C and C \cap D \subseteq D, so P(C \cap D) \leq \min(P(C), P(D)). Lower bound: P(C \cup D) \leq 1, and P(C \cup D) = P(C) + P(D) - P(C \cap D), so P(C \cap D) \geq P(C) + P(D) - 1. Combining with P(C \cap D) \geq 0 gives the bound. ∎

I.2 Corollary I.2 (Independence inside the bounds)

Statement. If C and D are independent, P(C \cap D) = p_C p_D. This value lies within the Fréchet–Hoeffding bounds whenever p_C p_D \geq \max(0, p_C + p_D - 1) and p_C p_D \leq \min(p_C, p_D), which is always true for p_C, p_D \in [0,1].

Proof. The two inequalities are elementary for p_C, p_D \in [0,1]. ∎

I.3 Remark I.3 (Tightness and use in TARA)

The Fréchet–Hoeffding bounds are distribution-free: they hold for any dependence structure. In TARA, when the joint physical model is available, the exact joint distribution is computed directly, and the bounds are not needed. The bounds are useful only when the joint model is infeasible to compute and only marginals are available. They then provide the range of possible joint probabilities but cannot distinguish between dependence structures within the range. Fréchet bounds must not be substituted for the physical joint model when the physical model is available; they are a fallback, not a substitute.

