Appendix L — Proof of Falsification-Condition Constructiveness

Statement. Each hypothesis H1–H7 in Chapter 3 is associated with a falsification condition that is constructible from the experimental protocol in Chapter 5, meaning there exists a finite scenario set and a finite decision procedure that can establish the falsification condition within the experimental budget.

Argument (constructive, not a proof of existence). For each hypothesis:

· H1 (granularity sufficiency): Falsification is the null result \eta_r^{mech} \geq \eta_r^{v0.2} for every receptor on the declared scenario set. Constructed by computing both \eta values under the empirical distribution induced by E1's scenarios. Finite scenario set, finite computation.
· H2 (decision-relevance of separation): Falsification is \Delta_{\mathrm{rev}} >  uncertainty band for every scenario. Constructed by computing \Delta_{\mathrm{rev}} against B1, B2, B3 in E4 and comparing to the Tier-1 + Tier-2 band. Finite scenario set, finite computation.
· H3 (joint dependence magnitude): Falsification is dependence statistically indistinguishable from independence for all receptor–asset pairs in E2. Constructed by the standard test for P(C \cap D) = P(C)P(D) under the specified confidence level. Finite scenario set, finite computation.
· H4 (trajectory-level detection): Falsification is terminal-state sufficiency for all modeled receptor mechanisms in E3. Constructed by comparing trajectory-based and terminal-state hazard indicators. Finite mechanism set, finite computation.
· H5 (model-form robustness changes admissibility): Falsification is admissibility invariance across all model forms in E5. Constructed by evaluating admissibility under each \Phi_r^{(l)} in \mathcal{M}_{\mathrm{form}}. Finite model set, finite computation.
· H6 (epistemic learning value): Falsification is \mathrm{VOR}_e = 0 for every investigation e and every policy under investigation in E7. Constructed by computing VOR for each candidate investigation. Finite investigation set, finite computation.
· H7 (negative-control invariance): Falsification is a decision reversal in the near-independence scenario. Constructed by the negative-control scenario specified in E6. Finite scenario, finite computation.

Caveat. Constructibility means the falsification procedure is well-defined and finite. It does not mean the computational budget is sufficient to reach statistical power. E4's power requirement (Appendix F.3) may exceed the available budget, in which case the experiment is reported as underpowered. The falsification condition is constructible in principle but may not be attainable in practice. This distinction is reported honestly.

